      "source_index": 788,
      "source_part_index": 0,
      "text": "The server is responding but routing isn't matching. The issue is the path parser — `parts[1]` for `GET / HTTP/1.1` will be `/`, but let me check the `_parse_request` path handling. The issue: `GET /api/status HTTP/1.1` — `parts[1]` = `/api/status` which should match. But the `Content-Length` isn't being parsed, so the body read might be blocking. Actually looking at the connection loop, we read until `\\r\\n\\r\\n` is found, but then pass the body as the remaining bytes. The HTTP body read works for this. The issue is likely that the `switch` in `api_dispatch` uses `strings.has_prefix` but the path `/api/status` case uses `req.path == \"/api/status\"` which should match.\n\nLet me check if it's actually serving on the right port — the \"not found\" JSON is our own response, so routing IS happening but not matching. The issue is `path` parsing when the request line has `HTTP/1.1` at the end — `parts[1]` should be just the path. Let me check with a simpler curl-like test:"
    },
